    {
      "id": "q1",
      "prompt": "A controller is stable for every gain in the continuous model of an integrator plant. After sampling with a zero-order hold and one sample of computation delay, why can a large gain become unstable?",
      "kind": "choice",
      "dimension": "C",
      "critical": true,
      "options": [
        "A digital controller cannot implement proportional gain exactly, so large gains saturate the arithmetic and diverge",
        "Sampling removes the plant integrator, so the discrete loop has no integral action and a high gain becomes unsafe",
        "The zero-order hold flips the sign of the feedback at every sample, so any gain at all becomes unstable",
        "The hold and the delay add lag (about 1.5 samples of delay), so a gain that is too high pushes against stale information"
      ],
      "answer": "3",
      "explanation": "Delay adds phase lag proportional to frequency; with high gain the loop crosses over where the lag exceeds the margin.",
      "knowledgeType": "concept",
      "skillTags": [
        "delay-stability"
      ],
      "skillLabel": "Stability limits with delay",
      "mistakeCategory": "conceptual",
      "remediationRefs": [
        {
          "label": "Review the lesson section",
          "href": "/lessons/C09#theory-and-derivation"
        }
      ],
      "relatedPrerequisites": [
        "C03",
        "M11"
      ]
    },
